# 4. COURSE DEVELOPMENT REQUIREMENTS

Every new course must be a real instructional experience.

Do not create empty course shells.

Do not use placeholder descriptions.

Do not create modules containing only titles.

Do not populate every course with the same generic lesson content.

Do not use repetitive, AI-generated filler simply to increase the amount of content.

Each course must contain the following.

## A. Course information

- Course title.
- Unique slug.
- Detailed description.
- Learning objectives.
- Competency association.
- Category association through the existing competency relationship.
- Difficulty level.
- Estimated duration.
- Target audience.
- Prerequisites where appropriate.
- Course cover image or thumbnail where supported.
- Course version.
- Publication status.
- Creation and update timestamps.

## B. Course curriculum

Each foundational course should normally contain 6 to 8 meaningful modules.

The number may differ where the competency requires a different structure.

Every module must contain:

- Module title.
- Module description.
- Learning objectives.
- Estimated duration.
- Display order.
- Publication status.

Modules must follow a logical progression from foundational knowledge to more advanced application.

## C. Lessons

Each module should normally contain 2 to 4 substantive lessons.

Each lesson must contain:

1. A clear title.
2. A detailed explanation of the topic.
3. Relevant examples.
4. Practical illustrations.
5. Step-by-step instructions where appropriate.
6. Key concepts and definitions.
7. Learning objectives.
8. A knowledge check or reflection where appropriate.
9. A practical activity where appropriate.
10. Supplementary resources.
11. Estimated completion time.
12. Clear completion criteria.

Lessons must teach something meaningful.

A heading followed by two sentences is not sufficient instructional content.

Write original, relevant educational material appropriate to the course level.

Use external documentation, videos and articles as supplementary resources rather than replacing the instructional content.

Where external resources are included, provide their title, source, URL and resource type.

Do not invent external URLs.

